#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167943} :activity/id #uuid "a585794a-f4e3-5afb-9bdf-f3bee0610206" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049306 :topic/id #uuid "eb34afb7-9644-525e-b42b-cf82816ee5e0" :topic/math-academy-id 3195} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167943 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168034 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will represent a $2\\pi$-periodic function $f(x)$ as an infinite sum of simple sine and cosine waves. This representation is called the **Fourier series** for $f(x)$:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\frac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big)\n$$\n\nThe key idea is that we can \"build\" a complicated periodic graph by adding together basic waves. For example, below we have a discontinuous periodic function approximated by the first few terms of its Fourier series.\n\n![](images/39/392a8df0fab00010e76036aa0fec2c60f25b5766b3cacc7983b7443d909866eb.png)\n\nThis gives us a powerful way to analyze periodic behavior by breaking it down into simple oscillations. Fourier series play a central role in modeling vibrations, signals, and other repeating patterns.\n\nTo work with Fourier series, we typically start with a function defined on a single base interval, such as $[-\\pi,\\pi),$ and then extend it to be periodic over all real numbers. This process creates what is called a **periodic extension**.\n\nIn the next slide, we will define this concept formally and look at some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "1d19e02d-99c0-5649-b7a3-bdb37c8e97e5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14624 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "822d573c-a967-52e4-854c-d2b5f14b0fff" :step/math-academy-id 600573 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167955}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167944 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166962 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166562 :question/id #uuid "5888d4ce-bc45-540b-afa0-901008abfcde" :question/math-academy-id "e-21787"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049091} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050172}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315069}] :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "c6f0ea8f-2008-5f49-84d9-7341157766fc" :step/math-academy-id 600574 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167945}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167945 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168036 :tutorial/content "Recall that we can define an inner product on functions over $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ given by\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x), g(x)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nAlso recall that functions $f$ and $g$ are called *orthogonal* (with respect to this inner product) if $\\langle f(x), g(x)\\rangle=0.$\n\nThe key fact we will use throughout this lesson is that the functions\n\n$$\n1,\\ \\cos(x),\\ \\sin(x),\\ \\cos(2x),\\ \\sin(2x),\\ \\ldots\n$$\n\nare orthogonal with respect to the inner product defined above.\n\nMore precisely, for integers $m,n\\ge 1,$ we have the following orthogonality relations on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$:\n\n- $\\langle \\cos(mx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0$ if $m\\ne n,$\n\n- $\\langle \\sin(mx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0$ if $m\\ne n,$ and\n\n- $\\langle \\sin(mx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0$ for all $m,n.$\n\nAlso, the constant function $1$ is orthogonal to every sine and cosine:\n\n- $\\langle 1,\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0,$ and\n\n- $\\langle 1,\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0$ for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nFinally, we will also use the squared norms of these functions:\n\n$$\n\\langle 1,1\\rangle=2\\pi,\n\\quad\n\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=\\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=\\pi\n\\quad\n\\text{for all } n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nLet's see how this works in concrete examples.\n\n**Note:** Both intervals $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ and $[0,2\\pi)$ are considered standard in this context. We’ll use $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ in tutorials, but some exercises use $[0,2\\pi).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9c1c7f37-624c-5c49-9ded-2b3dd3b37d7a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14639 :tutorial/title "Reminder: Orthogonal Functions"} :step/id #uuid "f80997bd-d9ed-5a35-b485-c34868575480" :step/math-academy-id 600578 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167946}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167946 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166963 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166563 :question/id #uuid "052f11a0-2f27-5285-83af-6d8fdb729024" :question/math-academy-id "e-21788"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fadf4f64-3569-56b2-98e1-f6681892054a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050331}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397472}] :knowledge-point/title "Checking Orthogonality of Sines and Cosines"} :step/id #uuid "b4a0ba44-f514-5920-a47f-39a8f107bb7d" :step/math-academy-id 600579 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167947}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167947 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168035 :tutorial/content "Recall that we are considering a $2\\pi$-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous on $[-\\pi,\\pi).$ We can represent $f(x)$ by its Fourier series:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big).\n$$\n\nOur goal is to find the formulas for the coefficients $a_0, a_n,$ and $b_n.$\n\nTo do this, we'll use a powerful idea from linear algebra. We define an *inner product* for functions on the interval $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ as\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x), g(x)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWith this inner product, the set of functions $\\{1, \\cos(nx), \\sin(nx)\\}$ for $n\\ge 1$ is an *orthogonal set*.\n\nThis means we can think of the Fourier coefficients as *projections* of the function $f(x)$ onto each of these orthogonal \"directions\". The coefficients $a_0,a_n,b_n$ simply tell us \"how much\" of $f(x)$ lies in each of these directions.\n\nOn the next slide, we will use this idea to derive the formula for the first coefficient, $a_0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "304c879d-2d08-5f1e-985b-938b67e64dfb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14628 :tutorial/title "Fourier Series Form For 2pi-Periodic Functions"} :step/id #uuid "769e9784-6852-5663-879d-f2dd13159e0a" :step/math-academy-id 600583 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167956}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167948 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166964 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166564 :question/id #uuid "d2127a8e-14bb-5574-a542-852333fe4cca" :question/math-academy-id "e-21789"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "304c015d-cbb6-5989-a102-d6f0f5f38604" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050841}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397539}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Expressions For Fourier Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "52c506ad-0e1c-5f71-b513-284e3650dd10" :step/math-academy-id 600584 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167949}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167949 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168043 :tutorial/content "Once again, recall that we are considering a $2\\pi$-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous on $[-\\pi,\\pi).$ We represent $f$ by its Fourier series\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big).\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients, we use the inner product on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$:\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x), g(x)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe have already seen the derivation for the coefficient $a_0$ early in the lesson.\n\nNow, we derive the formula for $a_n$ for $n\\ge 1{:}$\n\n- First, we take the inner product of both sides with $\\cos(nx){:}$\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle\n=\n\\left\\langle \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_k\\cos(kx)+b_k\\sin(kx)\\big),\\,\\cos(nx)\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\n- Using linearity, we get\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle\n=\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}\\langle 1,\\cos(nx)\\rangle\n\\!+\\! \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} a_k\\langle \\cos(kx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle\n\\!+\\! \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} b_k\\langle \\sin(kx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\n- By orthogonality, $\\langle 1,\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0,$ $\\langle \\sin(kx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0$ for all $k,$ and $\\langle \\cos(kx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0$ whenever $k\\ne n,$ so\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=a_n\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSince $\\displaystyle \\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} \\cos^2(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\pi,$ we obtain\n\n$$\na_n=\\dfrac{\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle}{\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, we derive the formula for $b_n$ for $n\\ge 1{:}$\n\n- First, we take the inner product of both sides with $\\sin(nx){:}$\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle\n=\n\\left\\langle \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_k\\cos(kx)+b_k\\sin(kx)\\big),\\,\\sin(nx)\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\n- Using linearity, we get\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle\n=\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}\\langle 1,\\sin(nx)\\rangle\n\\!+\\! \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} a_k\\langle \\cos(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle\n\\!+\\! \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} b_k\\langle \\sin(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\n- By orthogonality, $\\langle 1,\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0,$ $\\langle \\cos(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0$ for all $k,$ and $\\langle \\sin(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0$ whenever $k\\ne n,$ so\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=b_n\\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSince $\\displaystyle \\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} \\sin^2(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\pi,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nb_n=\\dfrac{\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle}{\\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\sin(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nPutting these together, the Fourier coefficients are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dc7b8526-b305-5026-8e7d-5e426bbe1a15" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14668 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formulas For the Fourier Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "4fc54380-13dd-5632-a907-a555a9ed5733" :step/math-academy-id 600587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167955 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168052 :tutorial/content "Recall that a function $f(x)$ is *$p$-periodic* if $f(x+p)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ \n\nNow, if $F(x)$ is defined on an interval $x \\in [a,b)$ of length $p,$ then its **$p$-periodic extension** $f$ is defined by the two properties:  \n\n- $f(x+p)=f(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x \\in [a,b).$\n\nFor example, let $F(x) = -x$ be defined on $x\\in [0,2).$ Now, suppose $f(x)$ is the $2$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,2)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ The graphs of $y=F(x)$ and $y=f(x)$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/fe/fe66db8a73acee21bbd5f7c95d7598b448cb678408d2f47754ee4c30aefd5b14.png)\n\n**Watch out!** We should always be aware of the difference between $F(x)$ and its extension $f(x).$ For instance, consider the function $F(x)=2\\sin(x)$ defined on $x \\in [0,\\pi)$ and its $\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x).$ The graphs of $y=F(x)$ and $y=f(x)$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/b1/b1299bb593554a930a12870d39c74aa8d23ea97027b9e9e253e8fda6b592e42a.png)\n\nNotice that $f(x)$ is not just $2\\sin(x)!$ But it would coincide with $2\\sin(x)$ if we were to consider the $2\\pi$-periodic extension of $F(x)=2\\sin(x)$ from, say, $x \\in [0,2\\pi]$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bbf1ed42-199b-56dd-ba70-e35c3597339a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14853 :tutorial/title "Periodic Extensions"} :step/id #uuid "c98bd153-6ef6-5953-a668-095e59197be9" :step/math-academy-id 606168 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167944}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167956 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168053 :tutorial/content "Let's derive the formula for the coefficient $a_0$ by taking the inner product of the entire Fourier series \n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big)\n$$\n\nwith the constant function $1.$\n\n- **Step 1.** Start with the Fourier series and take the inner product of both sides with $1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),1\\rangle\n=\n\\left\\langle \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big),\\,1\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Use the linearity of the inner product to distribute it across the sum:\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),1\\rangle\n=\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}\\langle 1,1\\rangle\n+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n\\langle \\cos(nx),1\\rangle\n+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} b_n\\langle \\sin(nx),1\\rangle\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Apply the orthogonality property. For any $n\\ge 1,$ we have $\\langle \\cos(nx),1\\rangle=0$ and $\\langle \\sin(nx),1\\rangle=0.$ This makes all the terms in the sums equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),1\\rangle=\\dfrac{a_0}{2}\\langle 1,1\\rangle\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Solve for $a_0.$ We just need to calculate $\\langle 1,1\\rangle=\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} 1\\cdot 1\\,\\textrm{d}x=2\\pi.$\n\n$$\na_0=\\dfrac{2\\langle f(x),1\\rangle}{\\langle 1,1\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{2\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\cdot 1\\,\\textrm{d}x}{2\\pi}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWe find the formulas for $a_n$ and $b_n$ (for $n\\ge 1$) in a similar way.\n\n- To find $a_n,$ we take the inner product of the Fourier series with $\\cos(nx).$ By orthogonality, the only term that is not zero on the right-hand side is the one involving $\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle.$\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle = a_n \\langle \\cos(nx), \\cos(nx)\\rangle\n$$\n\nSince $\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}\\cos^2(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\pi,$ we can solve for $a_n{:}$\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle}{\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\n- Similarly, to find $b_n,$ we take the inner product with $\\sin(nx){:}$\n\n$$\nb_n = \\dfrac{\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle}{\\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\sin(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\n**Note:** If $f$ is the $2\\pi$-periodic extension of a function $F$ defined on $[-\\pi,\\pi),$ then we can replace $f(x)$ with $F(x)$ inside the integrals.\n\nNow that we have the formulas for all the coefficients, let's work through a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "c5d2770f-53de-5939-9ec7-4155aef67173" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14854 :tutorial/title "The Formula For the Constant Term"} :step/id #uuid "a0bab88b-45ac-5613-b8cf-64b1eafabee1" :step/math-academy-id 606214 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167948}}] :activity/title "Introduction to Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166990}]}
